WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.639
Perpetual motion machines are badly named.

00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:05.950
A true perpetual motion machine doesn’t
just keep spinning forever:

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.340
if you were to set an object spinning out
in intergalactic space,

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.440
in some ideal true vacuum, it would keep spinning
forever.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.059
You just wouldn’t be able to get any useful
work out of it.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:19.570
If someone actually built a perpetual motion
machine, a true one,

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.660
it would give out more energy than is put
in:

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:25.369
enough to overcome friction, enough to overcome
air resistance,

00:00:25.369 --> 00:00:28.920
enough to keep going until mechanical failure.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:29.920
And like a wind turbine,

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.559
it’d let you take some of that extra energy
for your own use.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:36.890
There are, obviously, some physics objections
to this.

00:00:36.890 --> 00:00:41.010
The laws of thermodynamics always hold true.

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.520
This here is a device made by Thomas Young
in 1807.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.600
It is the original, two centuries old

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.550
from the archives of the Royal Institution
in London,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.579
which is why I have to wear gloves to touch
it.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:53.060
It’s an unbalanced wheel,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.440
and there have been many perpetual motion
machines like it.

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.239
There was a giant one in Los Angeles in the
early 20th century,

00:01:00.239 --> 00:01:02.899
advertising a café: and according to Popular
Mechanics,

00:01:02.899 --> 00:01:05.930
it kept going until there was a city-wide
power cut.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:09.189
And there was an inventor in 1829 who was
sure

00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:10.899
that a carriage with conical wheels

00:01:10.899 --> 00:01:15.310
would roll around the world “‘till time
shall be no more”.

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.149
They all work on the same principle:

00:01:17.149 --> 00:01:20.799
cheat the centre of gravity so it’s always
on one side,

00:01:20.799 --> 00:01:23.179
constantly unbalanced in the same direction.

00:01:23.179 --> 00:01:25.250
In this case, if you draw a line down the
middle,

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:27.729
there are more ball bearings on one side,

00:01:27.729 --> 00:01:29.359
so that side should be heavier,

00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:31.189
and when one gets near the top...

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:37.889
...it rolls over, so that side over there
is constantly being pushed down.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:38.889
This doesn’t work, obviously.

00:01:38.889 --> 00:01:41.090
And that’s why Thomas Young built this machine:

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:43.539
to demonstrate that it’s impossible.

00:01:43.539 --> 00:01:46.099
There may be more ball bearings on that side,

00:01:46.099 --> 00:01:48.990
but the ones on this side are further away
from the centre.

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:51.349
No matter how much you complicate it,

00:01:51.349 --> 00:01:53.030
this wheel will just rock back and forth

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:55.840
and eventually settle where the centre of
gravity is lowest,

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.279
or where friction stops it.

00:01:58.279 --> 00:02:02.099
But imagine if perpetual motion machines did
work.

00:02:02.099 --> 00:02:04.340
Which isn’t difficult to imagine, apparently:

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.979
there are all sorts of snake-oil companies

00:02:06.979 --> 00:02:11.650
who successfully sucker investors into giving
up surprising amounts of money,

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:15.040
and then never produce any actual proof of
a working prototype.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.920
There have been so many attempts that both
the US patent office and the UK "pay-tent" office

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.810
refuse to even entertain the idea of perpetual
motion machines.

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.340
Now, you can argue physics and thermodynamics
all you want,

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:32.250
but there’s a more common-sense argument
against perpetual motion machines,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:34.540
against the idea of free energy.

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.230
Because regardless of how it’s powered,

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.170
whether it’s an unbalanced wheel or something
else,

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:43.200
if you have a magic box that can give out
more energy than it takes in:

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.569
what happens if you use a small one to power
a bigger one,

00:02:46.569 --> 00:02:47.659
and so on, and so on, and so on?

00:02:47.659 --> 00:02:50.489
You could start with something the size of
a grain of sand,

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:52.890
and rotate the Earth with it.

00:02:52.890 --> 00:02:56.959
Or what happens if you plug it into itself?

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:00.409
If you have something that multiplies the
energy you put in...

00:03:00.409 --> 00:03:03.289
then after a few seconds, you’ve got a bomb.

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:09.580
Thank you very much to the Royal Institution!

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:13.219
Over on their channel I’m debunking three
other types of perpetual motion machines

00:03:13.219 --> 00:03:14.560
along with someone from their team.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:15.119
Go check it out!

